# Context

## Background
DB.md said that the busy timeout of 5 s makes a statement fail only
after that wait. The release runbook said that a batch of the running
hub service "fails after the same 5 s" during a migration. Neither was
true for the transactions of the hub store.

## Why This Way
A probe with the bundled SQLite (driver v1.14.53) showed that a
deferred transaction which reads before it writes gets "database is
locked" at once, under a lock held for 300 ms and under one held for
7 s. SQLite skips the busy handler for that change (lang_transaction,
section 2.1). The hub then makes three attempts in 302 ms. The
documents were corrected to the code on main, not the code to the
documents: the transaction mode is a decision for the owner (NOTES.md,
"Store transactions and the write lock").

## Gotchas
- The writer pool has one connection. A test that holds the write lock
  must open a second pool on the same file. A connection taken from the
  store's own pool blocks the store on the pool, not on the lock.
- The ingest journal shows 1,508 locked lines since 2026-11-16, 1,494
  of them in one hour on 2026-11-20. The journal does not name the
  other writer.

